the rbd or s1  response  on the madonna samples too we see a  strong rbd and s1 response and the  presence of at least one donor who  appears to be previously infected  thus we see a spread of igg responses  from donors  not all donors showing  high intensity  one can also study trends in antibody  development before and after first and  second shot of vaccination  in this study here we evaluated a few  donors  where we had samples from before  vaccination after the first vaccine and  after the second vaccine  as we see in the plots from these donors  there's different trends in igg response  on vaccination after the first and  second doses  but we do see that there is a boost  which is achieved after the second  vaccine  it also seems like the levels of igg  produced between the different donors  are also very different  this kind of data can be compared in the  plots  as shown in this figure  where the plots show the response  from each donor  for antibodies against each particular  antigen in the vaccination studies  we notice in each case that the rbd  response is dominant and is boosted  significantly  with the second shot  studies such as this in larger donor  pools might provide a more complete  picture of the trends on what's going on  with regard to the immune  response in these kind of different  sample types  i would like to next take a few minutes  and switch to discussing how flow  cytometry can provide answers relevant  to covet 19 research for cellular  questions  as we discussed earlier  covert 19 research can often require  quick screening of blood and pvmc  samples to obtain characterization and  enumeration of subpopulations  flow cytometry platforms that can  provide absolute count information  economically are very useful for the id  and enumeration of whole blood or pbmc  samples  those assays such as the count and  viability assay or immunology assays on  microcapillary platform can provide  answers using very low amount of samples  using assay specific templates  the counted mobility assay uses a  pre-formulated mix of membrane  penetrating and non-penetrating dyes and  this allows for the sensitive detection  of nucleated cells  dead cells and debris  and thus in the sample when mixed with  the cellular sample it can provide  distinction of viable cells from debris  and distinction of dead cells from  viable cells using very small quantities  of sample  similarly the muse  immunology assays are mix and read asses  that can provide information on  different immune cell populations in  whole blood or pbmc samples very easily  and quickly  closed captioning not  cell types available  fresh and frozen pbmc samples  the assay has a  dedicated software module that outputs  summarized data in an easy to interpret  format as shown in this figure  the cell concentration outputs from the  assay have been compared to predicate  methods  for fresh pvmcs frozen pbmcs and  multiple concentrations  and as you can see on this plot here  that you're seeing very nice  correlations between the different  methods  precision of the assay has also been  studied  and not only for pbmcs but also for  other cell lines and typically what we  observed is that you can obtain a  percent cb of less than 10 percent for  cell concentration and viability results  and thus this assay can be of great  utility uh in looking at viability or  concentrations of cellular samples that  might be  important especially if people are uh  you know preparing the samples for  immune response studies or prescreening  the samples and deciding whether it's  good to go to the next experiment  absolute counts and percentages of t and  b cells have shown to be very important  in the phenotyping of cov19 samples and  cd4 and cd8 t cells in particular have  been shown to undergo significant  reduction with the severity of the  disease  capturing this information for all  samples can be very important  and meals immunology assays can provide  absolute counts of cd4 and cd8 t cells  total lymphocyte counts and percentage  of population using only 10 microliter  of blood  example of cd4 t cell and cd8 t cell is  shown in this figure over here  dot plots show the results for the cd4 t  cell for example here where we can see  the upper  two populations represent the total  lymphocytes and the upper right  population represents the cd4 t cell  population  similarly for cd8 the upper right  represents the cda t cell and the total  upper population represents total  lymphocyte and this is utilized to  obtain information on absolute count of  the t cell  total lymphocyte count and percentage  as a percent of total lymphocytes  on the right here we show comparison of  the cd4 t cell absolute khans from muse  using microcapillary cytometry against  higher end flow platforms and as we look  at this figure we see that across a wide  range of measurements we are seeing  really nice correspondence of results  we've also looked at precision  on these assays  here is results from looking at three  operators on three instruments running  four samples and triplicates and as we  look at the percent cvs in these  measurements we see typically that we  obtain a percent cv of less than 10 for  a lot of these studies  thus the simple no wash protocol  and easy to use instrument with micro  capillary psychometry can really enable  one to quickly obtain  enumeration information on tnb cell  populations  one can also obtain more detailed  studies and obtain enumeration  information on multiple subpopulations  in parallel  here we show multiparametric analysis  for tb and k populations using flow  cytometry  example is shown for cd checks plus on  the top and cd checks low controls  stained with combinations of cd45  cd3  cd4 cd8 cd19 and cd56 and cd16  antibodies and most of these antibodies  are each conjugated a different  fluorophore and so can be looked at in  different channels  the data can be easily mined to obtain  count and percentage of each of these  populations  and  they can be then compared  with other  methods or other data that you've  obtained here for example we have  compared the results obtained to those  provided by the vendor  as shown in the plots on the right as  you can see in this graphs here  the the results from the user's sidewall  hd are in blue and the vendor results  and the limits are in different colors  and as you can see from the data of the  absolute count information for the  various subpopulation in both sample  types as well within limits provided by  the vendor  data such as this which can be obtained  in 10 microliters of blood uh it can be  very useful  and especially because you can perform  these assays in a no wash format in  either tubes or plates  one can take this further to obtain more  detailed information on knife and memory  t-cell populations  here fresh whole blood was stained with  different combinations of antibodies in  a known wash format and analyzed by  microcapillary flow psychometry  in addition to being able to dissect t  cells  one can see the b cells and k cells and  further one can subdivide the cd4 and  cd8 t cells  into  knive and memory sub populations using  additional antibodies in the mix  this information can also be obtained  with 10 microliters of blood and these  kind of phenotyping studies can thus be  very useful in studying different donors  and much greater detail  to summarize my presentation today  we discussed that cobin 19 research  requires the evaluation of a wide range  of cellular and serological parameters  and these methods can be enabled by flow  cytometry  we also discussed that serological  studies continue to be important for  understanding coping 19 related immune  response  its sustenance and the post-vaccination  response  we showed you information on the guava  sarskovi to multi-antigen antibody assay  and describe that it's a new  multi-antigen bead-based acid for flow  cytometry that can provide a complete  picture of antibodies against multiple  sarcobito antigens the targets include  nucleocapsid protein rbd and spike s1  protein  igg igm and iga antibodies against all  these proteins can be detected with  using the same kit  and the assay is a universal assay which  can be utilized on platforms like the  guava mu cell analyzer the guava easy  side systems as well as other flow  cytometers  we hope that with this increased  capability the combined information from  cellular and antibody characteristics on  the same flow cytometry platforms can  enhance and empower cover 19 research  and finally i would like to acknowledge  all team members and colleagues who  contributed to this work  the bulk of the data presented was  generated by kim van praan and julie  khloe  my thanks to my r d and marketing team  members who contributed to multiple  aspects of this project  and finally my thanks to the audience  for the attention  thank you  thank you kamala for your informative  presentation we will now start the q a  portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  let's get started  all right our first question  kamala is the information on isotopes  important when studying vaccinated  samples and what what role do these  isotopes play  thank you for the question and thank you  everybody for attending uh today's  webinar  so when you're looking at  source code infection natural infection  numerous studies have shown  that the different isotypes igp igm and  iga  are observed in serum and plasma samples  and and they play a very important role  in neutralizing activity for example  in vaccinated samples too studies are  showing uh in particular the presence of  iga  antibodies too several studies have  demonstrated that  and uh in fact some recent studies have  also shown the presence of iga not only  in serum but also in  a milk secretion they have shown in  shown that in mucosal  uh pathways so so  the isotypes are present and because  they contribute to the immunity in the  natural infection it's expected that  these antibody isotypes also  contribute to the neutralizing activity  that we see  even from the  vaccinated samples so this has become a  very important area of study where  information is still being gathered  because men you know  because uh  vaccination and then looking at the  samples and details are still newer  and um  and the answer type  and what do  how do they  really build up and contribute to that  resistance  and the immune response is still to be  understood  thank you  thank you next question we have here  what are the advantages in flow  cytometry over other methods for sars  kobe 2 serology  that's a good very good question there  are numerous methods for serology and  each of them has their own advantage  but flow cytometry provides certain  unique advantages  for um the biggest advantage you can get  by doing the assay by serology i say by  flow cytometry for example like we have  shown  is that you can take advantage of the  multiplexing that you can get with a  flow cytometry  you know you're able to look at multiple  response to multiple antigens in  parallel so there's a lot more  information that you're getting per  experiment  or per  run per assay that is run on the  circometer  secondly you have fluorescence detection  and many other methods for serology use  calorimetric methods uh for example  and these have lower some tend to have  lower sensitivity but  with  fluoresce with flow cytometry you get  the advantage of the high sensitivity  that can come from fluorescent detection  and that results in actually the  advantages of being able to detect  samples early on and it also  and also comes with a broad dynamic  range that is really important to look  at natural infection versus and as well  as vaccinated samples  so um  the third kind of advantage you have in  how we have the assay structured  especially by flow cytometry is that um  many other methods require you to run  plates or place those samples so  um so you need to so depending if you  had fewer samples you may have to  sacrifice certain  um  or certain uh your spots in your plate  but um in a flow cytometry if you had a  fewer samples you could run this in  tubes or you could run it in plates it  just depends on  what your throughput may be or how it  varies from day to day so certainly very  distinct advantages with flow  but the also another crucial example is  that a lot of people who are doing these  kind of assays are also interested in in  cellular immunity in addition to immoral  immunity  and a lot of those assays get run on  flow cytometers and now by having um  being having this capability to run  covet 19 serology and flow cytometry  they're able to do both their immoral  and immunity studies as well as cellular  immunity related studies on the same  platform  thank you for that informative answer  um our next question we have here why is  serology still so important in a  post-vaccine world  that's a very good question and in fact  the importance of serology keeps getting  more and more  important uh you know with vaccination  what people are looking for is what's  the immune response do we have are we do  we have the antibodies present in it  which are which many of which have  studies have established can  fight off infection or severe infection  right  so um with vaccines you know people are  interested in understanding a is there  is there that immune response then what  is the duration how long do these  antibodies stay on do they go down do  they need to be  uh get booster shots so all of these  kind of answers are aided by serological  assays  the other kind of information that you  can get is  with relation to  particular subgroups now numerous  studies  in the past several months have  established that  um certain subgroups uh  of people who maybe you know who have  had maybe kidney transplant cancer  or be on  immunosuppressive drugs  many of these  people have had reduced  or sometimes no level of antibodies and  this is  again extremely important to understand  um  what is what is their immune reaction  especially with regard to antibody  production is on vaccination and what  kind of strategies do we need to develop  so that we can bolster that response for  uh  for all people  um  and uh and you know there may be  particular treatments needed in certain  cases uh  for these kind of subpopulation so what  strategies will work better for them  so so there are a lot of open questions  still and i think serology plays a very  crucial role  in  trying to gather information and answer  some of these questions which  will help us with tackling the var uh  the kovin-19 and the virus itself  thank you it looks like we have time for  one more question  what is the utility of screening against  multiple antigens approach for sars kobe  2 serology  well that's a good question um  as we saw in the talk we had examples of  a multi-antigen approach  and if you look at it  what it really tells us is it gives us  information on the full spectrum  or on a more comprehensive spectrum of  antibodies that are present  in a sample that you study  uh but  in addition there is also this um  these scenarios where for example if  there was  someone who was um  who has not been had the uh coven 19 but  they had a vaccination especially with  anything  any vaccine strategies that related to  the spike protein then we can see in  those cases that we only see  antibodies related to the spike or the  receptor binding domain protein coming  up so  now if these people had natural  infection or  were you know were having natural  infection at the time  um samples were taken from them  you would see  also the nucleocapsid protein response  antibodies against the nucleocapsid  protein come up  so  so the having multiple antigens this  allows you to  learn a little bit more about the full  spectrum of antibodies as well as  distinguish certain scenarios  like uh in this case um  vaccination strategies with spike  proteins and probably without  infection  thank you so much kamala for your time  today and your important research before  we go i'd also like to thank the  audience for joining us today and their  interesting questions  and also our sponsor luminex for  sponsoring today's webinar  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand for  two years until september 9th of 2023  and labyrinths will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time thank  you so much again kamala goodbye  thank you everyone
 

